1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,486
Two series, two hundred quarters. They rise together and fall together,

2
00:00:04,586 --> 00:00:07,458
and a regression of one on the other gives a t

3
00:00:07,558 --> 00:00:11,916
statistic of twenty-five, far beyond one point nine six. A discovery?

4
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:13,656
Both were made by flipping

5
00:00:13,756 --> 00:00:16,628
coins, independently. Nothing connects them.

6
00:00:17,966 --> 00:00:20,569
Today: how a shock travels from one series

7
00:00:20,669 --> 00:00:23,979
to another, and when moving together means something.

8
00:00:25,066 --> 00:00:29,402
Start with a vector autoregression: money growth and inflation, each

9
00:00:29,502 --> 00:00:33,120
regressed on last quarter's values of both. Stand them up

10
00:00:33,220 --> 00:00:36,251
as two rows of dominoes, one domino per quarter.

11
00:00:36,333 --> 00:00:40,457
A money shock tips the first domino. Because we let money move

12
00:00:40,557 --> 00:00:44,409
first, inflation tips too, by a half, in the same quarter.

13
00:00:44,500 --> 00:00:48,292
That ordering is an assumption, not a fact found in the data.

14
00:00:48,366 --> 00:00:51,483
Then each domino pushes the next one in its own row,

15
00:00:51,583 --> 00:00:55,319
and the one diagonally across. The ripple fades as it travels.

16
00:00:55,800 --> 00:00:59,121
Stand each tilt up as a height, and the dominoes become

17
00:00:59,221 --> 00:01:03,103
the impulse responses. The numbers show what the ripple is: each

18
00:01:03,203 --> 00:01:06,462
column is the previous one multiplied by the matrix A.

19
00:01:06,533 --> 00:01:10,211
Multiply eight times and you have the whole response. Both

20
00:01:10,311 --> 00:01:13,989
eigenvalues of A, nought point seven and nought point two,

21
00:01:14,089 --> 00:01:16,464
are below one, so the shock dies away.

22
00:01:17,533 --> 00:01:21,236
Back to the coin-flip pairs. Repeat the experiment a thousand

23
00:01:21,336 --> 00:01:24,852
times and collect the t statistics. The textbook says they

24
00:01:24,952 --> 00:01:28,093
should sit inside the red band. They don't. The test

25
00:01:28,193 --> 00:01:31,522
declares a relation that is not there nearly four times

26
00:01:31,622 --> 00:01:35,013
in five, and more data only makes the illusion stronger.

27
00:01:35,100 --> 00:01:38,603
Take first differences and the problem disappears: the same test

28
00:01:38,703 --> 00:01:41,530
then rejects five percent of the time, as it should.

29
00:01:42,600 --> 00:01:45,866
Now a drunk walks home with her dog. Each of them

30
00:01:45,966 --> 00:01:50,400
wanders at random; neither path is stationary. But the leash keeps

31
00:01:50,500 --> 00:01:55,209
the distance between them bounded. That is cointegration: y minus beta

32
00:01:55,309 --> 00:01:58,095
x is stationary, although x and y are not.

33
00:01:58,166 --> 00:02:03,222
Put a stranger beside her, walking independently. Their distance wanders off

34
00:02:03,322 --> 00:02:07,699
without limit; any leash between them would snap. In economics the

35
00:02:07,799 --> 00:02:12,652
leashes are consumption and income, long and short interest rates, prices

36
00:02:12,752 --> 00:02:16,925
at home and abroad. Engle and Granger's test asks exactly this:

37
00:02:17,025 --> 00:02:20,453
is the residual of the levels regression stationary?

38
00:02:21,533 --> 00:02:24,022
So: in a VAR, a shock travels like a line

39
00:02:24,122 --> 00:02:27,370
of dominoes, and the impulse response is the trace it

40
00:02:27,470 --> 00:02:31,096
leaves. Two random walks can look related and mean nothing;

41
00:02:31,196 --> 00:02:34,886
they are cointegrated only when a leash holds them together.

